---
// THE FIGURE, and the only drawing of it in the site's markup — a hollow ring and a solid disc
// with a rule between them. Design and build, two things, one line; the rule is the
// relationship, and the ring-vs-disc is which half.
//
// HOLLOW RING, SOLID DISC (chooser: andrewshiau-mono-redesign, Q7/02 — "unfilled → filled").
// Both dots used to be solid, in the two duality hues, and the only thing distinguishing them
// was colour: navy and olive are 2.14:1 apart in greyscale (STYLE.md), so at a glance, in a
// screenshot, or to a colourblind reader the figure was two identical dots. Outline vs. fill is
// the signal now — it survives greyscale, a 16px favicon, and a photocopier — which is why the
// figure is drawn in `currentColor` throughout rather than in `--design` / `--build`.
//
// THE RULE IS TRIMMED AT BOTH ENDS (chooser: andrewshiau-mark-options, Q1/04). It used to run
// centre to centre, (10,10)→(22,22), which was invisible while both dots were solid: the line's
// ends were buried under the fills. Hollow the leading dot and the buried end reappears inside
// the ring as a stub poking through it. Trimmed, the rule spans the gap and touches neither
// object, so both keep their own edge and the rule reads as a third element — a dimension line
// between two things rather than a stick joining them.
// The numbers are the geometry, not taste. The join runs at 45°, so the unit vector along it is
// (0.70711, 0.70711). The ring's OUTER edge is r plus half its stroke — 5 + 0.9 = 5.9 — so the
// rule starts at 10 + 5.9(0.70711) = 14.17. The disc's near edge is r, so it ends at
// 22 − 5(0.70711) = 18.46. Change r or the stroke and both numbers move.
// The cost, accepted: the trimmed rule is 4.29 units of a 32-unit box, so it renders about a
// tenth of the figure's width. At the 18px row marker that is ~2.4px of line. It is the
// smallest mark on the page and it is meant to be — see `.side .mark` in global.css for why 18
// is the floor (below it the 1.8 ring stroke drops under one device pixel and the ring greys).
//
// It was inline in Mark.astro until the case-study marker started drawing it too (chooser:
// andrewshiau-panel-marks, Q5/06 — the logo goes and the marker becomes the link home). Two
// hand copies of one figure is the drift PanelHead.astro exists to prevent, so the figure is
// its own file. `Mark.astro` — the 44px panel masthead — is gone: the figure's job on the page
// is now the index's row marker (chooser: andrewshiau-mark-options, Q3/05), so every drawing of
// it that ships carries a fact. Its two wrappers are `SideDot.astro` (the index row marker) and
// `SideMark.astro` (the case-study marker, which is also the link home).
//
// SIDE-AWARE, which is the point of splitting it out. A page or a row that sits on ONE half of
// the practice has no relationship to draw, so it gets that half's own object — a ring for
// design, a disc for build — rather than a figure whose whole subject is a pairing that isn't
// there. It is centred at the midpoint of the diagonal the pair spans, so the one-sided mark is
// the two-sided mark with the pairing removed rather than a different glyph.
//
// r=7.2, not the pair's r=5, and the number is measured rather than derived. At r=5 the single
// dot paints 47% of the ink the pair does inside the same 26px box — less than half the weight
// for the FIRST thing on the page, so a one-sided panel head read lighter than a two-sided one
// and the eight heads stopped being one system.
// Measured by rendering each candidate at 4× and summing coverage, because arithmetic on the
// radius gets this wrong: r=9 looks like "18px of ink to match 18px of ink" by bounding box and
// paints 151% of the pair. Coverage against the pair: r=5 47%, r=7 91%, r=7.2 ~95%, r=7.5 105%,
// r=9 151%. 7.2 is the closest match on the light side — a hair under is right for a solid disc
// against a figure that is mostly outline. Those measurements were taken when both dots were
// filled, so they now describe the DISC exactly and the ring approximately; the ring is lighter
// than 95% by however much its hole removes, which is the correct direction — a ring that
// matched a disc's ink would be a doughnut, not an outline.
//
// The other copy of this figure is `public/favicon.svg`, which cannot import anything and
// carries its own hexes in a `<style>` block. If the geometry here changes, change it there.
import type { Side } from '../data/experiments';
interface Props { side?: Side }
const { side = 'both' } = Astro.props;
---
{side === 'both' ? (
  <svg class="mark" viewBox="0 0 32 32" aria-hidden="true" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
    <line x1="14.17" y1="14.17" x2="18.46" y2="18.46" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>
    <circle cx="10" cy="10" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>
    <circle cx="22" cy="22" r="5" fill="currentColor"/>
  </svg>
) : side === 'build' ? (
  <svg class="mark mark-one" viewBox="0 0 32 32" aria-hidden="true" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
    <circle cx="16" cy="16" r="7.2" fill="currentColor"/>
  </svg>
) : (
  <svg class="mark mark-one" viewBox="0 0 32 32" aria-hidden="true" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
    <circle cx="16" cy="16" r="7.2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>
  </svg>
)}
